Data Cleaning & Preprocessing Summary

2.1 Data Cleaning and Preparation
To ensure that the analysis accurately reflects retail investor attention and discussion patterns on WallStreetBets during the 2020–2021 period, we implemented a structured data cleaning and preprocessing pipeline focused on improving data consistency, removing redundancy, and preparing features relevant to engagement analysis.

First, the time-related variables were standardized by converting the timestamps into datetime format and comparing it with the original unix time field. Redundant time columns were removed, and additional temporal features such as posting hour, month, and day of week were extracted to support analysis of when investor discussions are most active.
Second, we conducted a comprehensive duplicate and near-duplicate assessment. This included checking for repeated rows, duplicate post IDs, and repeated titles. In addition, semantic similarity checks based on TF-IDF and cosine similarity were used to detect highly similar posts that could artificially inflate engagement patterns. These steps help ensure that observed discussion trends are not driven by duplicated or redundant content.
For textual data, the title and post body were cleaned to improve consistency and reduce noise. The cleaning process included converting text to lowercase, removing URLs and markdown artifacts, decoding HTML entities, and normalizing whitespace. These steps allow more reliable extraction of textual features and prevent formatting issues from affecting the analysis.
Missing values were analyzed both visually and statistically. Since a large proportion of posts do not contain body text, this missing is treated as a structural missing rather than a random missing. We create a binary indicator variable (has_body) to capture whether the post contains additional text content and replace missing body values with empty strings to maintain compatibility with text processing. This approach preserves potentially meaningful differences between short posts and posts that have been discussed in depth.
Due to the presence of viral posts, engagement variables such as post score and number of comments exhibited heavy-tailed distributions. We explored multiple outlier handling approaches—including IQR-based detection, Z-score diagnostics, and regression-based influence measures such as Cook’s distance—and compared several remediation strategies. Instead of removing influential observations, we applied log transformations (log1p) to stabilize variance while preserving the behavioral signals associated with viral investment discussions. The transformed variables were validated using distribution diagnostics such as Q–Q plots and statistical tests.
Finally, additional preprocessing steps were applied to improve model readiness and clarity of analytical. These included checking for near-zero variance features, examining multicollinearity using Spearman correlation, grouping rare post-type categories into a consolidated “Other” class, and preparing NLP-ready text fields for potential downstream analysis.

Overall, these preprocessing steps improve data quality while preserving meaningful signals about how retail investors discuss and respond to investment ideas on WallStreetBets.

2.2 Techniques
The data cleaning pipeline incorporated a broad range of techniques covering data validation, duplicate detection, text normalization, missing-data diagnostics, outlier detection, and feature preprocessing. In total, more than thirty techniques were applied, including TF-IDF similarity checks, MNAR diagnostics for missing text, multiple outlier detection methods (IQR, Z-score, Cook’s distance), and log transformations validated using Q–Q plots and statistical tests.